.TH MDT 1 "2026-10-21" "mdt 0.3.20" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mdt \- read Markdown in the terminal, with images, tables and maths
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdt
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIfile.md\fR
.br
.B mdt
[\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-\fR            (read the document from stdin)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdt
renders a Markdown document for the terminal.  It draws pictures and typeset
formulas with the kitty graphics protocol, iTerm2 inline images or sixel,
whichever the terminal supports, and falls back to text (a Unicode
transcription of each formula) when there is no graphics protocol at all.
The maths engine is built in \- MathJax's TeX to SVG converter running inside
QuickJS \- so no LaTeX installation is needed.
.PP
The keymap follows \fBless\fR(1):
.BR j ", " k ", " Down ", " Up
scroll, Space and
.B b
page, 
.B g
and
.B G
jump to the top and the bottom,
.B J
and
.B K
move between headings,
.B Tab
opens the outline panel (a list longer than the panel shows a \fB12/47\fR counter and
an arrow, and \fBJ\fR/\fBK\fR, the wheel or a click walk it; \fBEnter\fR jumps to the
selection),
.B /
searches and
.BR n ", " N
walk the hits,
.B t
cycles the theme,
.B m
toggles maths,
.B e
copies the document as text,
.B p
switches the page renderer (see \fB\-\-render\fR) on and off,
.B r
reloads the file (with \fB\-\-watch\fR this happens by itself),
.B o
opens the first link (a click on a link opens a dialog that can copy or open
it),
.B ?
shows the help panel and
.B q
quits.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Graphics
.TP
.B \-\-gfx=auto|kitty|iterm2|sixel|none
Graphics protocol.  The default probes the terminal at start\-up.
.TP
.B \-\-math=katex|unicode|off
Typeset formulas as bitmaps (default), transcription only, or not at all.
.TP
.B \-\-render=text|image
\fBimage\fR is the page renderer, experimental: mdt draws the whole screen
itself \- text in a monospace font of its own, pictures and formulas at their
exact pixel position, level 1 and 2 headings at twice the size \- and sends it
through the graphics protocol, one strip per text row.  Only rows that changed
are sent, and a row the terminal has seen recently is reused, so scrolling a
line costs one new row.  It needs a graphics protocol and the cell size in
pixels (\fB\-\-cell\fR when the terminal does not report it); without them mdt
says so in the status bar and stays with text.  Behind a Windows console host
(ConPTY) the strips do not survive, as with any picture.  \fB\-\-screenshot\fR
honours it too.  The default is \fBtext\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-font\-px=N
Override the derived formula font size.
.TP
.B \-\-line\-numbers
Line numbers inside code blocks.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-syntax, \-\-syntax
Turn code highlighting off or on.
.SS Appearance
.TP
.B \-\-theme=dark|light|nord|monochrome
Colour theme (default: dark).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Monochrome output; equivalent to setting \fBNO_COLOR\fR in the environment.
.TP
.B \-\-toc
Open the outline panel on start.
.TP
.B \-\-panels
Tinted backgrounds behind code blocks, quotes and table headers.
.TP
.B \-\-terminal\-bg
Keep the terminal's own background colour.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-scrollbar
Hide the scrollbar.
.SS Source handling
.TP
.B \-\-encoding=auto|utf\-8|gbk|gb18030|big5|cp1252|latin1|off
How to read a file that is not UTF\-8.  The default,
.BR auto ,
converts a file whose bytes are not valid UTF\-8 from GB18030 (the "ANSI" code
page of a Chinese Windows).  GB18030's byte space is a superset of Big5's, so a
Big5 document has to be named explicitly; \fBoff\fR leaves the bytes alone.
.TP
.B \-\-entities=off|force
Repair a source that was HTML\-escaped before it reached you (off by default).
.TP
.B \-\-escapes=auto|off|force
Undo Markdown backslash escapes a generator left in the text.
.TP
.B \-\-loose=auto|off|on
Accept "#heading", "\- item" and "1.item" written without the space CommonMark
requires.
.TP
.B \-\-lang=auto|en|zh
Interface language (default: auto).  With \fBzh\fR the help text, the errors, the
settings file's complaints and the status bar are in Chinese.  The order is
\fB\-\-lang\fR, then \fBMDT_LANG\fR, then \fBlang = zh\fR in the settings file,
then \fBLC_ALL\fR/\fBLC_MESSAGES\fR/\fBLANG\fR.  A language mdt does not speak is
an error rather than a quiet fallback.  \fBzh_TW\fR and \fBzh_HK\fR get the same
(simplified) text.
.TP
.B \-\-diag
Print what mdt sees of the file (encoding, the repairs that were applied, the
block census, the first characters as code points, the memory the document
costs), which input path it is using (\fBwindows console records\fR,
\fBwindows byte stream\fR or \fBposix tty (bytes)\fR), the interface language
it chose and which setting chose it, and of the
terminal.  Works without a tty, so the output can be pasted
into a bug report.
.TP
.B \-\-compat
Turn off everything optional \- graphics, DEC 2026 synchronized output, the
keyboard\-protocol push, the capability probe \- for terminals that mishandle
them.
.SS Reading
.TP
.B \-\-watch, \-\-no\-watch
Reload the document whenever the file changes on disk; \fB\-\-no\-watch\fR turns
it back off, and \fBwatch = off\fR does the same from the settings file.  The
reload keeps the reader's place in the document.  Saving from an editor is the
case it is built for, including the temporary\-file\-and\-rename save most
editors use, and a file next to the document changing is not a reason to
reload.  Only the interactive reader can do this: with \fB\-\-dump\fR,
\fB\-\-screenshot\fR or \fB\-\-diag\fR, and when reading stdin, \fB\-\-watch\fR
is an error rather than a flag that quietly does nothing.
.SS Non\-interactive
.TP
.B \-\-dump[=file]
Print the document as plain text (no graphics) and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-screenshot=out.png
Render the screen to a PNG with the built\-in font rasteriser and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-width=N, \-\-height=N
Size for \fB\-\-dump\fR and \fB\-\-screenshot\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-cell=WxH
Cell size in pixels for \fB\-\-dump\fR and \fB\-\-screenshot\fR (default 9x19).
.TP
.B \-\-code\-fit
Code frames hug the code instead of spanning the page.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-caps
Print the detected terminal capabilities and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help, \-v, \-\-version
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/mdt/config
Defaults, one "key = value" per line: \fBtheme\fR, \fBgfx\fR, \fBmath\fR,
\fBencoding\fR, \fBpanels\fR, \fBterminal_bg\fR, \fBscrollbar\fR,
\fBline_numbers\fR, \fBsyntax\fR, \fBcode_fit\fR, \fBwatch\fR, \fBrender\fR.  The command line overrides
the file.  On Windows the file is \fI%APPDATA%\\mdt\\config\fR; the environment
variable \fBMDT_CONFIG\fR points somewhere else.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set (to anything), the monochrome theme is used \- unless a theme is given
on the command line or in the settings file, which is the more specific request.
.TP
.B MDT_CONFIG
Path of the settings file.
.TP
.B MDT_FRAME_LOG
Write every frame to this file exactly as it was sent, escape sequences spelled
out, to tell "mdt drew it wrong" apart from "the terminal lost it".
.TP
.B MDT_KEY_LOG
Append every chunk of input mdt receives to this file, hex and ASCII side by
side, one line per read.  For a report of the shape "it exits when I press an
arrow key", this is what tells "nothing arrived" apart from "it arrived in
pieces" \- on Windows the console decides that, and hosts differ.
.TP
.B MDT_FORCE_CURL_BIN
Use this program instead of \fBcurl\fR to fetch a remote picture.
.SH NOTES
.TP
.B Pictures on Windows
A Windows console host (ConPTY) does not pass long escape sequences through: it
draws them as text, wrapped at the console width.  mdt therefore draws pictures
there only when the terminal itself answered the capability probe;
\fB\-\-gfx=\fR forces a protocol for hosts that do pass sequences through.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The input file could not be read (missing file, a directory, unreadable, larger
than 512 MB, ...).
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong; the message names the values that would have
worked.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR less (1),
.BR bat (1),
.BR glow (1)
.PP
